description:
src/memory.rs:275-282 declares:

```
/// Set the maximum Sieve script size in bytes for size enforcement tests.
///
/// When set, `SieveBackend::max_sieve_script_bytes` returns this limit,
/// causing `handle_sieve_set` to reject scripts exceeding it with `tooLarge`.
#[cfg(feature = "sieve")]
pub fn set_max_sieve_script_bytes(&self, limit: u64) {
    self.inner.lock().unwrap().max_sieve_script_limit = Some(limit);
}
```

The docstring states it is intended "for size enforcement tests" — but the method is `pub` without `#[doc(hidden)]`. A consumer reading `cargo doc` sees this as a recommended way to configure the reference impl's Sieve size limit, when the actual design intent is "use this in test fixtures only; production should impl `SieveBackend::max_sieve_script_bytes` themselves on their real backend".

The crate-level memory-module doc already says "Not production." globally, but a per-method `#[doc(hidden)]` or a more explicit "Test-only — use a real `SieveBackend` for production" warning on this specific method would prevent confusion.

The same critique applies to `register_account` and `store_blob` (lines 263, 270), though those have legitimate use in example/demo code beyond pure tests. `set_max_sieve_script_bytes` is more clearly test-only because production backends would derive the limit from per-account quota state, not from a global mutator.

Priority P2 because: `feature = "memory"` already carries a "not production" doc-level warning and pre-1.0 stability disclaimer. But the per-method intent is unclear to a doc-only reader.

Suggested fix: either `#[doc(hidden)]` the test-only knobs, OR rewrite the docstring to make the test-only intent the first sentence:

```
/// **Test-only**: set the maximum Sieve script size in bytes. Production
/// backends should implement `SieveBackend::max_sieve_script_bytes` directly
/// on their own backend type.
```
